## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about streaming similarity with limited memory, a practical beginner-level challenge that still touches core complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- MinHash sketches for Jaccard similarity
- Batch processing costs vs per-element costs
- Trade-offs: d (accuracy) vs memory, impact of collisions
- Exact vs approximate counting implications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: initialize sketches for A and B
class MinHash {
  constructor(d, maxHash) { this.d = d; this.sig = Array(d).fill(Infinity); this.maxHash = maxHash; }
  add(x) { for (let i=0; i<this.d; i++) { const h = hash_i(x, i) % this.maxHash; if (h < this.sig[i]) this.sig[i] = h; } }
  resembles(other) { let matches = 0; for (let i=0; i<this.d; i++) if (this.sig[i] === other.sig[i]) matches++; return matches / this.d; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you choose d given target accuracy ε?
- How handle skewed data or duplicates in batches?
- How would you extend to weighted or multi-set similarity?

## Why This Is Asked
Streaming frequency sketches are common in prod telemetry. This question probes knowledge of CMS, accuracy guarantees, batch processing, and amortized space-time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Count-Min Sketch (CMS)
- ε, δ error bounds
- Update/query complexity for batches
- Space complexity O(wd)
- Trade-offs vs exact counting

## Code Example
```javascript
// CMS skeleton
const w = Math.ceil(Math.E / eps);
const d = Math.ceil(Math.log(1/delta));
const table = Array.from({length: d}, () => Array(w).fill(0));

function update(x) { for (let i = 0; i < d; i++) table[i][hash_i(i, x) % w]++; }

function query(x) { let m = Infinity; for (let i = 0; i < d; i++) m = Math.min(m, table[i][hash_i(i, x) % w]); return m; }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would deletions or non-monotone streams affect CMS choices?
- How would you monitor actual error in production without degrading performance?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design online dynamic order-statistics with batch inserts, focusing on tangible metrics (inversions) and practical data structures, not abstract theory.

## Key Concepts

- Inversions, Fenwick tree, coordinate compression, online batch processing, duplicates handling, memory trade-offs.

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.bit=new Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  update(i,delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i&-i) this.bit[i]+=delta; }
  query(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i&-i) s+=this.bit[i]; return s; }
}
// assume valueToRank and rankToValue are defined, U is number of ranks
let bit = new Fenwick(U);
let totalInv = 0;
let seen = 0;
for (const x of batch){
  const r = valueToRank[x];
  totalInv += seen - bit.query(r);
  bit.update(r, 1);
  seen++;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle deletions or updates to previous elements?
- How would you adapt if the value domain is unbounded or values arrive online without prior compression?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of amortized analysis with a practical growth strategy, highlighting how linear growth increments behave differently from exponential doubling and how memory copies interact with cache locality.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized cost under linear growth
- Block-based capacity increments (capacity increases by B, not a multiplicative factor)
- Cache locality during bulk copies
- Trade-offs between growth frequency and per-copy work

## Code Example
```javascript
class Queue {
  constructor(capacity = B) {
    this.buffer = new Array(capacity);
    this.head = 0;
    this.tail = 0;
    this.size = 0;
  }

  enqueue(item) {
    if (this.size === this.buffer.length) {
      const newBuffer = new Array(this.buffer.length + B);
      for (let i = 0; i < this.size; i++) {
        newBuffer[i] = this.buffer[(this.head + i) % this.buffer.length];
      }
      this.buffer = newBuffer;
      this.head = 0;
      this.tail = this.size;
    }
    this.buffer[this.tail] = item;
    this.tail = (this.tail + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size++;
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this.size === 0) return undefined;
    const item = this.buffer[this.head];
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size--;
    return item;
  }
}
```

## B's Influence
- **Total copies**: Larger B reduces growth frequency, cutting total copy work from O(N²) toward O(N) (since copies ≈ N²/(2B))
- **Cache behavior**: Smaller B keeps working set in cache but triggers more frequent expensive copies; larger B improves sequential access patterns during copies but may evict useful cache contents when reallocating

